Page 8   BASIC M6502 8K VER 1.1 BY MICRO-SOFT
          SOME EXPLANATION.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
0000:                      .subttl SOME EXPLANATION.
0000:              
0000:              ; M6502 BASIC CONFIGURES BASIC AS FOLLOWS
0000:              
0000:              ; LOW LOCATIONS
0000:              ; 	PAGE	ZERO
0000:              
0000:              ; 		STARTUP:
0000:              ; 		INITIALLY A JMP TO INITIALIZATION CODE BUT
0000:              ; 		CHANGED TO A JMP TO "READY".
0000:              ; 		RESTARTING THE MACHINE AT LOC 0 DURING PROGRAM
0000:              ; 		EXECUTION CAN LEAVE THINGS MESSED UP.
0000:              
0000:              ; 		LOC OF FAC TO INTEGER AND INTEGER TO FAC
0000:              ; 		ROUTINES.
0000:              
0000:              ; 		"DIRECT" MEMORY:
0000:              ; 		THESE ARE THE MOST COMMONLY USED LOCATIONS.
0000:              ; 		THEY HOLD BOOKKEEPING INFO AND ALL OTHER
0000:              ; 		FREQUENTLY USED INFORMATION.
0000:              ; 		ALL TEMPORARIES, FLAGS, POINTERS, THE BUFFER AREA,
0000:              ; 		THE FLOATING ACCUMULATOR, AND ANYTHING ELSE THAT
0000:              ; 		IS USED TO STORE A CHANGING VALUE SHOULD BE LOCATED
0000:              ; 		IN THIS AREA. CARE MUST BE MADE IN MOVING LOCATIONS
0000:              ; 		IN THIS AREA SINCE THE JUXTAPOSITION OF TWO LOCATIONS
0000:              ; 		IS OFTEN DEPENDED UPON.
0000:              
0000:              ; 		STILL IN RAM WE HAVE THE BEGINNING OF THE "CHRGET"
0000:              ; 		SUBROUTINE. IT IS HERE SO [TXTPTR] CAN BE THE
0000:              ; 		EXTENDED ADDRESS OF A LOAD INSTRUCTION.
0000:              ; 		THIS SAVES HAVING TO BOTHER ANY REGISTERS.
0000:              
0000:              ; 	PAGE	ONE
0000:              ; 		THE STACK.
0000:              
0000:              ; 	STORAGE PAGE TWO AND ON
0000:              ; 		IN RAM VERSIONS THESE DATA STRUCTURES COME AT THE
0000:              ; 		END OF BASIC. IN ROM VERSON THEY ARE AT RAMLOC WHICH
0000:              ; 		CAN EITHER BE ABOVE OR BELOW ROMLOC, WHICH IS WHERE
0000:              ; 		BASIC ITSELF RESIDES.
0000:              
0000:              ; 				A ZERO.
0000:              ; 		[TXTTAB]	POINTER TO NEXT LINE'S POINTER.
0000:              ; 				LINE # OF THIS LINE (2 BYTES).
0000:              ; 				CHARACTERS ON THIS LINE.
0000:              ; 				ZERO.
0000:              ; 				POINTER AT NEXT LINE'S POINTER
0000:              ; 					(POINTED TO BY THE ABOVE POINTER).
0000:              ; 				... REPEATS ...
0000:              ; 		LAST LINE:	POINTER AT ZERO POINTER.
0000:              ; 				LINE # OF THIS LINE.
0000:              ; 				CHARACTERS ON THIS LINE.
0000:              ; 				ZERO.
0000:              ; 				DOUBLE ZERO (POINTED TO BY THE ABOVE POINTER).
0000:              ; 		[VARTAB]	SIMPLE VARIABLES. 6 BYTES PER VALUE.
0000:              ; 				2 BYTES GIVE THE NAME, 4 BYTES THE VALUE.
0000:              ; 				... REPEATS ...
0000:              ; 		[ARYTAB]	ARRAY VARIABLES. 2 BYTES NAME, 2 BYTE
0000:              ; 				LENGTH, NUMBER OF DIMENSIONS , EXTENT OF
0000:              ; 				EACH DIMENSION (2BYTES/), VALUES

Page 9   BASIC M6502 8K VER 1.1 BY MICRO-SOFT
          SOME EXPLANATION.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
0000:              ; 				... REPEATS ...
0000:              ; 		[STREND]	FREE SPACE.
0000:              ; 				... REPEATS ...
0000:              ; 		[FRETOP]	STRING SPACE IN USE.
0000:              ; 				... REPEATS ...
0000:              ; 		[MEMSIZ]	HIGHEST MACHINE LOCATION.
0000:              ; 				UNUSED EXCEPT BY THE VAL FUNCTION.
0000:              
0000:              ; 		ROM -- CONSTANTS AND CODE.
0000:              
0000:              ; 	FUNCTION DISPATCH ADDRESSES (AT ROMLOC)
0000:              ; 		"FUNDSP" CONTAINS THE ADDRESSES OF THE
0000:              ; 		FUNCTION ROUTINES IN THE ORDER OF THE
0000:              ; 		FUNCTION NAMES IN THE CRUNCH LIST.
0000:              ; 		THE FUNCTIONS THAT TAKE MORE THAN ONE ARGUMENT
0000:              ; 		ARE AT THE END. SEE THE EXPLANATION AT "ISFUN".
0000:              
0000:              ; 	THE OPERATOR LIST
0000:              ; 		THE "OPTAB" LIST CONTAINS AN OPERATOR'S PRECEDENCE
0000:              ; 		FOLLOWED BY THE ADDRESS OF THE ROUTINE TO PERFORM
0000:              ; 		THE OPERATION. THE INDEX INTO THE
0000:              ; 		OPERATOR LIST IS MADE BY SUBTRACTING OFF THE CRUNCH VALUE
0000:              ; 		OF THE LOWEST NUMBERED OPERATOR. THE ORDER
0000:              ; 		OF OPERATORS IN THE CRUNCH LIST AND IN "OPTAB" IS IDENTICAL.
0000:              ; 		THE PRECEDENCES ARE ARBITRARY EXCEPT FOR THEIR
0000:              ; 		COMPARATIVE SIZES. NOTE THAT THE PRECEDENCE FOR
0000:              ; 		UNARY OPERATORS SUCH AS "NOT" AND NEGATION ARE
0000:              ; 		SETUP SPECIALLY WITHOUT USING THE LIST.
0000:              
0000:              ; 	THE RESERVED WORD OR CRUNCH LIST
0000:              ; 		WHEN A COMMAND OR PROGRAM LINE IS TYPED IN
0000:              ; 		IT IS STORED IN "BUF". AS SOON AS THE WHOLE LINE
0000:              ; 		HAS BEEN TYPED IN ("INLIN" RETURNS) "CRUNCH" IS
0000:              ; 		CALLED TO CONVERT ALL RESERVED WORDS TO THEIR
0000:              ; 		CRUNCHED VALUES. THIS REDUCES THE SIZE OF THE
0000:              ; 		PROGRAM AND SPEEDS UP EXECUTION BY ALLOWING
0000:              ; 		LIST DISPATCHES TO PERFORM FUNCTIONS, STATEMENTS,
0000:              ; 		AND OPERATIONS. THIS IS BECAUSE ALL THE STATEMENT
0000:              ; 		NAMES ARE STORED CONSECUTIVELY IN THE CRUNCH LIST.
0000:              ; 		WHEN A MATCH IS FOUND BETWEEN A STRING
0000:              ; 		OF CHARACTERS AND A WORD IN THE CRUNCH LIST
0000:              ; 		THE ENTIRE TEXT OF THE MATCHED WORD IS TAKEN OUT OF
0000:              ; 		THE INPUT LINE AND A RESERVED WORD TOKEN IS PUT
0000:              ; 		IN ITS PLACE. A RESERVED WORD TOKEN IS ALWAYS EQUAL
0000:              ; 		TO OCTAL 200 PLUS THE POSITION OF THE MATCHED WORD
0000:              ; 		IN THE CRUNCH LIST.
0000:              
0000:              ; 	STATEMENT DISPATCH ADDRESSES
0000:              ; 		WHEN A STATEMENT IS TO BE EXECUTED, THE FIRST
0000:              ; 		CHARACTER OF THE STATEMENT IS EXAMINED
0000:              ; 		TO SEE IF IT IS LESS THAN THE RESERVED
0000:              ; 		WORD TOKEN FOR THE LOWEST NUMBERED STATEMENT NAME.
0000:              ; 		IF SO, THE "LET" CODE IS CALLED TO
0000:              ; 		TREAT THE STATEMENT AS AN ASSIGNMENT STATEMENT.
0000:              ; 		OTHERWISE A CHECK IS MADE TO MAKE SURE THE
0000:              ; 		RESERVED WORD NUMBER IS NOT TOO LARGE TO BE A
0000:              ; 		STATEMENT TYPE NUMBER. IF NOT THE ADDRESS
0000:              ; 		TO DISPATCH TO IS FETCHED FROM "STMDSP" (THE STATEMENT
0000:              ; 		DISPATCH LIST) USING THE RESERVED WORD
0000:              ; 		NUMBER FOR THE STATEMENT TO CALCULATE AN INDEX INTO

Page 10   BASIC M6502 8K VER 1.1 BY MICRO-SOFT
          SOME EXPLANATION.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
0000:              ; 		THE LIST.
0000:              
0000:              ; 	ERROR MESSAGES
0000:              ; 		WHEN AN ERROR CONDITION IS DETECTED,
0000:              ; 		[ACCX] MUST BE SET UP TO INDICATE WHICH ERROR
0000:              ; 		MESSAGE IS APPROPRIATE AND A BRANCH MUST BE MADE
0000:              ; 		TO "ERROR". THE STACK WILL BE RESET AND ALL
0000:              ; 		PROGRAM CONTEXT WILL BE LOST. VARIABLES
0000:              ; 		VALUES AND THE ACTUAL PROGRAM REMAIN INTACT.
0000:              ; 		ONLY THE VALUE OF [ACCX] IS IMPORTANT WHEN
0000:              ; 		THE BRANCH IS MADE TO ERROR. [ACCX] IS USED AS AN
0000:              ; 		INDEX INTO "ERRTAB" WHICH GIVES THE TWO
0000:              ; 		CHARACTER ERROR MESSAGE THAT WILL BE PRINTED ON THE
0000:              ; 		USER'S TERMINAL.
0000:              
0000:              ; 	TEXTUAL MESSAGES
0000:              ; 		CONSTANT MESSAGES ARE STORED HERE. UNLESS
0000:              ; 		THE CODE TO CHECK IF A STRING MUST BE COPIED
0000:              ; 		IS CHANGED THESE STRINGS MUST BE STORED ABOVE
0000:              ; 		PAGE ZERO, OR ELSE THEY WILL BE COPIED BEFORE
0000:              ; 		THEY ARE PRINTED.
0000:              
0000:              ; 	FNDFOR
0000:              ; 		MOST SMALL ROUTINES ARE FAIRLY SIMPLE
0000:              ; 		AND ARE DOCUMENTED IN PLACE. "FNDFOR" IS
0000:              ; 		USED FOR FINDING "FOR" ENTRIES ON
0000:              ; 		THE STACK. WHENEVER A "FOR" IS EXECUTED, A
0000:              ; 		16-BYTE ENTRY IS PUSHED ONTO THE STACK.
0000:              ; 		BEFORE THIS IS DONE, HOWEVER, A CHECK
0000:              ; 		MUST BE MADE TO SEE IF THERE
0000:              ; 		ARE ANY "FOR" ENTRIES ALREADY ON THE STACK
0000:              ; 		FOR THE SAME LOOP VARIABLE. IF SO, THAT "FOR" ENTRY
0000:              ; 		AND ALL OTHER "FOR" ENTRIES THAT WERE MADE AFTER IT
0000:              ; 		ARE ELIMINATED FROM THE STACK. THIS IS SO A
0000:              ; 		PROGRAM THAT JUMPS OUT OF THE MIDDLE
0000:              ; 		OF A "FOR" LOOP AND THEN RESTARTS THE LOOP AGAIN
0000:              ; 		AND AGAIN WON'T USE UP 18 BYTES OF STACK
0000:              ; 		SPACE EVERY TIME. THE "NEXT" CODE ALSO
0000:              ; 		CALLS "FNDFOR" TO SEARCH FOR A "FOR" ENTRY WITH
0000:              ; 		THE LOOP VARIABLE IN
0000:              ; 		THE "NEXT". AT WHATEVER POINT A MATCH IS FOUND
0000:              ; 		THE STACK IS RESET. IF NO MATCH IS FOUND A
0000:              ; 		"NEXT WITHOUT FOR"  ERROR OCCURS. GOSUB EXECUTION
0000:              ; 		ALSO PUTS A 5-BYTE ENTRY ON STACK.
0000:              ; 		WHEN A RETURN IS EXECUTED "FNDFOR" IS
0000:              ; 		CALLED WITH A VARIABLE POINTER THAT CAN'T
0000:              ; 		BE MATCHED. WHEN "FNDFOR" HAS RUN
0000:              ; 		THROUGH ALL THE "FOR" ENTRIES ON THE STACK
0000:              ; 		IT RETURNS AND THE RETURN CODE MAKES
0000:              ; 		SURE THE ENTRY THAT WAS STOPPED
0000:              ; 		ON IS A GOSUB ENTRY. THIS ASSURES THAT
0000:              ; 		IF YOU GOSUB TO A SECTION OF CODE
0000:              ; 		IN WHICH A FOR LOOP IS ENTERED BUT NEVER
0000:              ; 		EXITED THE RETURN WILL STILL BE
0000:              ; 		ABLE TO FIND THE MOST RECENT
0000:              ; 		GOSUB ENTRY. THE "RETURN" CODE ELIMINATES THE
0000:              ; 		"GOSUB" ENTRY AND ALL "FOR" ENTRIES MADE AFTER
0000:              ; 		THE GOSUB ENTRY.
0000:              
0000:              ; 	NON-RUNTIME STUFF

Page 11   BASIC M6502 8K VER 1.1 BY MICRO-SOFT
          SOME EXPLANATION.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
0000:              ; 		THE CODE TO INPUT A LINE, CRUNCH IT, GIVE ERRORS,
0000:              ; 		FIND A SPECIFIC LINE IN THE PROGRAM,
0000:              ; 		PERFORM A "NEW", "CLEAR", AND "LIST" ARE
0000:              ; 		ALL IN THIS AREA. GIVEN THE EXPLANATION OF
0000:              ; 		PROGRAM STORAGE SET FORTH ABOVE, THESE ARE
0000:              ; 		ALL STRAIGHTFORWARD.
0000:              
0000:              ; 	NEWSTT
0000:              ; 		WHENEVER A STATEMENT FINISHES EXECUTION IT
0000:              ; 		DOES A "RTS" WHICH TAKES
0000:              ; 		EXECUTION BACK TO "NEWSTT". STATEMENTS THAT
0000:              ; 		CREATE OR LOOK AT SEMI-PERMANENT STACK ENTRIES
0000:              ; 		MUST GET RID OF THE RETURN ADDRESS OF "NEWSTT" AND
0000:              ; 		JMP TO "NEWSTT" WHEN DONE. "NEWSTT" ALWAYS
0000:              ; 		CHRGETS THE FIRST CHARACTER AFTER THE STATEMENT
0000:              ; 		NAME BEFORE DISPATCHING. WHEN RETURNING
0000:              ; 		BACK TO "NEWSTT" THE ONLY THING THAT
0000:              ; 		MUST BE SET UP IS THE TEXT POINTER IN
0000:              ; 		"TXTPTR". "NEWSTT" WILL CHECK TO MAKE SURE
0000:              ; 		"TXTPTR" IS POINTING TO A STATEMENT TERMINATOR.
0000:              ; 		IF A STATEMENT SHOULDN'T BE PERFORMED UNLESS
0000:              ; 		IT IS PROPERLY FORMATTED (I.E. "NEW") IT CAN
0000:              ; 		SIMPLY DO A RETURN AFTER READING ALL OF
0000:              ; 		ITS ARGUMENTS. SINCE THE ZERO FLAG
0000:              ; 		BEING OFF INDICATES THERE IS NOT
0000:              ; 		A STATEMENT TERMINATOR "NEWSTT" WILL
0000:              ; 		DO THE JMP TO THE "SYNTAX ERROR"
0000:              ; 		ROUTINE. IF A STATEMENT SHOULD BE STARTED
0000:              ; 		OVER IT CAN DO LDWD OLDTXT, STWD TXTPTR RTS SINCE THE TEXT PNTR
0000:              ; 		AT "NEWSTT" IS ALWAYS STORED IN "OLDTXT".
0000:              ; 		THE ^C CODE STORES [CURLIN] (THE
0000:              ; 		CURRENT LINE NUMBER) IN "OLDLIN" SINCE THE ^C CHECK
0000:              ; 		IS MADE BEFORE THE STATEMENT POINTED TO IS
0000:              ; 		EXECUTED. "STOP" AND "END" STORE THE TEXT POINTER
0000:              ; 		FROM "TXTPTR", WHICH POINTS AT THEIR TERMINATING
0000:              ; 		CHARACTER, IN "OLDTXT".
0000:              
0000:              ; 	STATEMENT CODE
0000:              ; 		THE INDIVIDUAL STATEMENT CODE COMES
0000:              ; 		NEXT. THE APPROACH USED IN EXECUTING EACH
0000:              ; 		STATEMENT IS DOCUMENTED IN THE STATEMENT CODE
0000:              ; 		ITSELF.
0000:              
0000:              ; 	FRMEVL, THE FORMULA EVALUATOR
0000:              ; 		GIVEN A TEXT POINTER POINTING TO THE STARTING
0000:              ; 		CHARACTER OF A FORMULA, "FRMEVL"
0000:              ; 		EVALUATES THE FORMULA AND LEAVES
0000:              ; 		THE VALUE IN THE FLOATING ACCUMULATOR (FAC).
0000:              ; 		"TXTPTR" IS RETURNED POINTING TO THE FIRST CHARACTER
0000:              ; 		THAT COULD NOT BE INTERPRETED AS PART OF THE
0000:              ; 		FORMULA. THE ALGORITHM USES THE STACK
0000:              ; 		TO STORE TEMPORARY RESULTS:
0000:              
0000:              ; 			0. PUT A DUMMY PRECEDENCE OF ZERO ON
0000:              ; 				THE STACK.
0000:              ; 			1. READ LEXEME (CONSTANT,FUNCTION,
0000:              ; 				VARIABLE,FORMULA IN PARENS)
0000:              ; 				AND TAKE THE LAST PRECEDENCE VALUE
0000:              ; 				OFF THE STACK.
0000:              ; 			2. SEE IF THE NEXT CHARACTER IS AN OPERATOR.

Page 12   BASIC M6502 8K VER 1.1 BY MICRO-SOFT
          SOME EXPLANATION.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
0000:              ; 				IF NOT, CHECK PREVIOUS ONE. THIS MAY CAUSE
0000:              ; 				OPERATOR APPLICATION OR AN ACTUAL
0000:              ; 				RETURN FROM "FRMEVL".
0000:              ; 			3. IF IT IS, SEE WHAT PRECEDENCE IT HAS
0000:              ; 				AND COMPARE IT TO THE PRECEDENCE
0000:              ; 				OF THE LAST OPERATOR ON THE STACK.
0000:              ; 			4. IF = OR LESS REMEMBER THE OPERATOR
0000:              ; 				POINTER OF THIS OPERATOR
0000:              ; 				AND BRANCH TO "QCHNUM" TO CAUSE
0000:              ; 				APPLICATION OF THE LAST OPERATOR.
0000:              ; 				EVENTUALLY RETURN TO STEP 2
0000:              ; 				BY RETURNING TO JUST AFTER "DOPREC".
0000:              ; 			5. IF GREATER PUT THE LAST PRECEDENCE
0000:              ; 				BACK ON, SAVE THE OPERATOR ADDRESS,
0000:              ; 				CURRENT TEMPORARY RESULT,
0000:              ; 				AND PRECEDENCE AND RETURN TO STEP 1.
0000:              
0000:              ; 		RELATIONAL OPERATORS ARE ALL HANDLED THROUGH
0000:              ; 		A COMMON ROUTINE. SPECIAL
0000:              ; 		CARE IS TAKEN TO DETECT TYPE MISMATCHES SUCH AS 3+"F".
0000:              
0000:              ; 	EVAL -- THE ROUTINE TO READ A LEXEME
0000:              ; 		"EVAL" CHECKS FOR THE DIFFERENT TYPES OF
0000:              ; 		ENTITIES IT IS SUPPOSED TO DETECT.
0000:              ; 		LEADING PLUSES ARE IGNORED,
0000:              ; 		DIGITS AND "." CAUSE "FIN" (FLOATING INPUT)
0000:              ; 		TO BE CALLED. FUNCTION NAMES CAUSE THE
0000:              ; 		FORMULA INSIDE THE PARENTHESES TO BE EVALUATED
0000:              ; 		AND THE FUNCTION ROUTINE TO BE CALLED. VARIABLE
0000:              ; 		NAMES CAUSE "PTRGET" TO BE CALLED TO GET A POINTER
0000:              ; 		TO THE VALUE, AND THEN THE VALUE IS PUT INTO
0000:              ; 		THE FAC. AN OPEN PARENTHESIS CAUSES "FRMEVL"
0000:              ; 		TO BE CALLED (RECURSIVELY), AND THE ")" TO
0000:              ; 		BE CHECKED FOR. UNARY OPERATORS (NOT AND
0000:              ; 		NEGATION)  PUT THEIR PRECEDENCE ON THE STACK
0000:              ; 		AND ENTER FORMULA EVALUATION AT STEP 1, SO
0000:              ; 		THAT EVERYTHING UP TO AN OPERATOR GREATER THAN
0000:              ; 		THEIR PRECEDENCE OR THE END OF THE FORMULA
0000:              ; 		WILL BE EVALUATED.
0000:              
0000:              ; 	DIMENSION AND VARIABLE SEARCHING
0000:              ; 		SPACE IS ALLOCATED FOR VARIABLES AS THEY ARE
0000:              ; 		ENCOUNTERED. THUS "DIM" STATEMENTS MUST BE
0000:              ; 		EXECUTED TO HAVE EFFECT. 6 BYTES ARE ALLOCATED
0000:              ; 		FOR EACH SIMPLE VARIABLE, WHETHER IT IS A STRING,
0000:              ; 		NUMBER OR USER DEFINED FUNCTION. THE FIRST TWO
0000:              ; 		BYTES GIVE THE NAME OF THE VARIABLE AND THE LAST FOUR
0000:              ; 		GIVE ITS VALUE. [VARTAB] GIVES THE FIRST LOCATION
0000:              ; 		WHERE A SIMPLE VARIABLE NAME IS FOUND AND [ARYTAB]
0000:              ; 		GIVES THE LOCATION TO STOP SEARCHING FOR SIMPLE
0000:              ; 		VARIABLES. A "FOR" ENTRY HAS A TEXT POINTER
0000:              ; 		AND A POINTER TO A VARIABLE VALUE SO NEITHER
0000:              ; 		THE PROGRAM OR THE SIMPLE VARIABLES CAN BE
0000:              ; 		MOVED WHILE THERE ARE ACTIVE "FOR" ENTRIES ON THE STACK.
0000:              ; 		USER DEFINED FUNCTION VALUES ALSO CONTAIN
0000:              ; 		POINTERS INTO SIMPLE VARIABLE SPACE SO NO USER-DEFINED
0000:              ; 		FUNCTION VALUES CAN BE RETAINED IF SIMPLE VARIABLES
0000:              ; 		ARE MOVED. ADDING A SIMPLE VARIABLE IS JUST
0000:              ; 		ADDING SIX TO [ARYTAB] AND [STREND], BLOCK TRANSFERING
0000:              ; 		THE ARRAY VARIABLES UP BY SIX AND MAKING SURE THE

Page 13   BASIC M6502 8K VER 1.1 BY MICRO-SOFT
          SOME EXPLANATION.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
0000:              ; 		NEW [STREND] IS NOT TOO CLOSE TO THE STRINGS.
0000:              ; 		THIS MOVEMENT OF ARRAY VARIABLES MEANS
0000:              ; 		THAT NO POINTER TO AN ARRAY WILL STAY VALID WHEN
0000:              ; 		NEW SIMPLE VARIABLES CAN BE ENCOUNTERED. THIS IS
0000:              ; 		WHY ARRAY VARIABLES ARE NOT ALLOWED FOR "FOR"
0000:              ; 		LOOP VARIABLES. SETTING UP A NEW ARRAY VARIABLE
0000:              ; 		MERELY INVOLVES BUILDING THE DESCRIPTOR,
0000:              ; 		UPDATING [STREND], AND MAKING SURE THERE IS
0000:              ; 		STILL ENOUGH ROOM BETWEEN [STREND] AND STRING SPACE.
0000:              ; 		"PTRGET", THE ROUTINE WHICH RETURNS A POINTER
0000:              ; 		TO A VARIABLE VALUE, HAS TWO IMPORTANT FLAGS. ONE IS
0000:              ; 		"DIMFLG" WHICH INDICATES WHETHER "DIM" CALLED "PTRGET"
0000:              ; 		OR NOT. IF SO, NO PRIOR ENTRY FOR THE VARIABLE IN
0000:              ; 		QUESTION SHOULD BE FOUND, AND THE INDEX INDICATES
0000:              ; 		HOW MUCH SPACE TO SET ASIDE. SIMPLE VARIABLES CAN
0000:              ; 		BE "DIMENSIONED", BUT THE ONLY EFFECT WILL BE TO
0000:              ; 		SET ASIDE SPACE FOR THE VARIABLE IF IT HASN'T BEEN
0000:              ; 		ENCOUNTERED YET. THE OTHER IMPORTANT FLAG IS "SUBFLG"
0000:              ; 		WHICH INDICATES WHETHER A SUBSCRIPTED VARIABLE SHOULD BE
0000:              ; 		ALLOWED IN THE CURRENT CONTEXT. IF [SUBFLG] IS NON-ZERO
0000:              ; 		THE OPEN PARENTHESIS FOR A SUBSCRIPTED VARIABLE
0000:              ; 		WILL NOT BE SCANNED BY "PTRGET", AND "PTRGET" WILL RETURN
0000:              ; 		WITH A TEXT POINTER POINTING TO THE "(", IF
0000:              ; 		THERE WAS ONE.
0000:              ; 	STRINGS
0000:              ; 		IN THE VARIABLE TABLES STRINGS ARE STORED JUST LIKE
0000:              ; 		NUMERIC VARIABLES. SIMPLE STRINGS HAVE THREE VALUE
0000:              ; 		BYTES WHICH ARE INITIALIZED TO ALL ZEROS (WHICH
0000:              ; 		REPRESENTS THE NULL STRING). THE ONLY DIFFERENCE
0000:              ; 		IN HANDLING IS THAT WHEN "PTRGET" SEES A "$" AFTER THE
0000:              ; 		NAME OF A VARIABLE, "PTRGET" SETS [VALTYP]
0000:              ; 		TO NEGATIVE ONE AND TURNS
0000:              ; 		ON THE MSB (MOST-SIGNIFIGANT-BIT) OF THE VALUE OF
0000:              ; 		THE FIRST CHARACTER OF THE VARIABLE NAME.
0000:              ; 		HAVING THIS BIT ON IN THE NAME OF THE VARIABLE ENSURES
0000:              ; 		THAT THE SEARCH ROUTINE WILL NOT MATCH
0000:              ; 		'A' WITH 'A$' OR 'A$' WITH 'A'. THE MEANING OF
0000:              ; 		THE THREE VALUE BYTES ARE:
0000:              ; 			LOW
0000:              ; 				LENGTH OF THE STRING
0000:              ; 				LOW 8 BITS
0000:              ; 				HIGH 8 BITS  OF THE ADDRESS
0000:              ; 					OF THE CHARACTERS IN THE
0000:              ; 					STRING IF LENGTH.NE.0.
0000:              ; 					MEANINGLESS OTHERWISE.
0000:              ; 			HIGH
0000:              ; 		THE VALUE OF A STRING VARIABLE (THESE 3 BYTES)
0000:              ; 		IS CALLED THE STRING DESCRIPTOR TO DISTINGUISH
0000:              ; 		IT FROM THE ACTUAL STRING DATA. WHENEVER A
0000:              ; 		STRING CONSTANT IS ENCOUNTERED IN A FORMULA OR AS
0000:              ; 		PART OF AN INPUT STRING, OR AS PART OF DATA, "STRLIT"
0000:              ; 		IS CALLED, CAUSING A DESCRIPTOR TO BE BUILT FOR
0000:              ; 		THE STRING. WHEN ASSIGNMENT IS MADE TO A STRING POINTING INTO
0000:              ; 		"BUF" THE VALUE IS COPIED INTO STRING SPACE SINCE [BUF]
0000:              ; 		IS ALWAYS CHANGING.
0000:              
0000:              ; 		STRING FUNCTIONS AND THE ONE STRING OPERATOR "+"
0000:              ; 		ALWAYS RETURN THEIR VALUES IN STRING SPACE.
0000:              ; 		ASSIGNING A STRING A CONSTANT VALUE IN A PROGRAM
0000:              ; 		THROUGH A "READ" OR ASSIGNMENT STATEMENT

Page 14   BASIC M6502 8K VER 1.1 BY MICRO-SOFT
          SOME EXPLANATION.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
0000:              ; 		WILL NOT USE ANY STRING SPACE SINCE
0000:              ; 		THE STRING DESCRIPTOR  WILL POINT INTO THE
0000:              ; 		PROGRAM ITSELF. IN GENERAL, COPYING IS DONE
0000:              ; 		WHEN A STRING VALUE IS IN "BUF", OR IT IS IN STRING
0000:              ; 		SPACE AND THERE IS AN ACTIVE POINTER TO IT.
0000:              ; 		THUS F$=G$ WILL CAUSE COPYING IF G$ HAS ITS
0000:              ; 		STRING DATA IN STRING SPACE. F$=CHR$(7)
0000:              ; 		WILL USE ONE BYTE OF STRING SPACE TO STORE THE
0000:              ; 		NEW ONE CHARACTER STRING CREATED BY "CHR$", BUT
0000:              ; 		THE ASSIGNMENT ITSELF WILL CAUSE NO COPYING SINCE
0000:              ; 		THE ONLY POINTER AT THE NEW STRING IS A
0000:              ; 		TEMPORARY DESCRIPTOR CREATED BY "FRMEVL" WHICH WILL
0000:              ; 		GO AWAY AS SOON AS THE ASSIGNMENT IS DONE.
0000:              ; 		IT IS THE NATURE OF GARBAGE COLLECTION THAT
0000:              ; 		DISALLOWS HAVING TWO STRING DESCRIPTORS POINT TO THE SAME
0000:              ; 		AREA IN STRING SPACE. STRING FUNCTIONS AND OPERATORS
0000:              ; 		MUST PROCEED AS FOLLOWS:
0000:              ; 			1) FIGURE OUT THE LENGTH OF THEIR RESULT.
0000:              
0000:              ; 			2) CALL "GETSPA" TO FIND SPACE FOR THEIR
0000:              ; 			RESULT. THE ARGUMENTS TO THE FUNCTION
0000:              ; 			OR OPERATOR MAY CHANGE SINCE GARBAGE COLLECTION
0000:              ; 			MAY BE INVOKED. THE ONLY THING THAT CAN
0000:              ; 			BE SAVED DURING THE CALL TO "GETSPA" IS A POINTER
0000:              ; 			TO THE DESCRIPTORS OF THE ARGUMENTS.
0000:              ; 			3) CONSTRUCT THE RESULT DESCRIPTOR IN "DSCTMP".
0000:              ; 			"GETSPA" RETURNS THE LOCATION OF THE AVAILABLE
0000:              ; 			SPACE.
0000:              ; 			4) CREATE THE NEW VALUE BY COPYING PARTS
0000:              ; 			OF THE ARGUMENTS OR WHATEVER.
0000:              ; 			5) FREE UP THE ARGUMENTS BY CALLING "FRETMP".
0000:              ; 			6) JUMP TO "PUTNEW" TO GET THE DESCRIPTOR IN
0000:              ; 			"DSCTMP" TRANSFERRED INTO A NEW STRING TEMPORARY.
0000:              
0000:              ; 		THE REASON FOR STRING TEMPORARIES IS THAT GARBAGE
0000:              ; 		COLLECTION HAS TO KNOW ABOUT ALL ACTIVE STRING DESCRIPTORS
0000:              ; 		SO IT KNOWS WHAT IS AND ISN'T IN USE. STRING TEMPORARIES ARE
0000:              ; 		USED TO STORE THE DESCRIPTORS OF STRING EXPRESSIONS.
0000:              
0000:              ; 		INSTEAD OF HAVING AN ACTUAL VALUE STORED IN THE
0000:              ; 		FAC, AND HAVING THE VALUE OF A TEMPORARY RESULT
0000:              ; 		BEING SAVED ON THE STACK, AS HAPPENS WITH NUMERIC
0000:              ; 		VARIABLES, STRINGS HAVE THE POINTER TO A STRING DESCRIPTOR
0000:              ; 		STORED IN THE FAC, AND IT IS THIS POINTER
0000:              ; 		THAT GETS SAVED ON THE STACK BY FORMULA EVALUATION.
0000:              ; 		STRING FUNCTIONS CANNOT FREE THEIR ARGUMENTS UP RIGHT
0000:              ; 		AWAY SINCE "GETSPA" MAY FORCE
0000:              ; 		GARBAGE COLLECTION AND THE ARGUMENT STRINGS
0000:              ; 		MAY BE OVER-WRITTEN SINCE GARBAGE COLLECTION
0000:              ; 		WILL NOT BE ABLE TO FIND AN ACTIVE POINTER TO
0000:              ; 		THEM. FUNCTION AND OPERATOR RESULTS ARE BUILT IN
0000:              ; 		"DSCTMP" SINCE STRING TEMPORARIES ARE ALLOCATED
0000:              ; 		(PUTNEW) AND DEALLOCATED (FRETMP) IN A FIFO ORDERING
0000:              ; 		(I.E. A STACK) SO THE NEW TEMPORARY CANNOT
0000:              ; 		BE SET UP UNTIL THE OLD ONE(S) ARE FREED. TRYING
0000:              ; 		TO BUILD A RESULT IN A TEMPORARY AFTER
0000:              ; 		FREEING UP THE ARGUMENT TEMPORARIES COULD RESULT
0000:              ; 		IN ONE OF THE ARGUMENT TEMPORARIES BEING OVERWRITTEN
0000:              ; 		TOO SOON BY THE NEW RESULT.
0000:              

Page 15   BASIC M6502 8K VER 1.1 BY MICRO-SOFT
          SOME EXPLANATION.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
0000:              ; 		STRING SPACE IS ALLOCATED AT THE VERY TOP
0000:              ; 		OF MEMORY. "MEMSIZ" POINTS BEYOND THE LAST LOCATION OF
0000:              ; 		STRING SPACE. STRINGS ARE STORED IN HIGH LOCATIONS
0000:              ; 		FIRST. WHENEVER STRING SPACE IS ALLOCATED (GETSPA).
0000:              ; 		[FRETOP], WHICH IS INITIALIZED TO [MEMSIZ], IS UPDATED
0000:              ; 		TO GIVE THE HIGHEST LOCATION IN STRING SPACE
0000:              ; 		THAT IS NOT IN USE. THE RESULT IS THAT
0000:              ; 		[FRETOP] GETS SMALLER AND SMALLER, UNTIL SOME
0000:              ; 		ALLOCATION WOULD MAKE [FRETOP] LESS THAN OR EQUAL TO
0000:              ; 		[STREND]. THIS MEANS STRING SPACE HAS RUN INTO THE
0000:              ; 		THE ARRAYS AND THAT GARBAGE COLLECTION MUST BE CALLED.
0000:              
0000:              ; 		GARBAGE COLLECTION:
0000:              ; 			0. [MINPTR]=[STREND] [FRETOP]=[MEMSIZ]
0000:              ; 			1. [REMMIN]=0
0000:              ; 			2. FOR EACH STRING DESCRIPTOR
0000:              ; 			(TEMPORARIES, SIMPLE STRINGS, STRING ARRAYS)
0000:              ; 			IF THE STRING IS NOT NULL AND ITS POINTER IS
0000:              ; 			.GT.MINPTR AND .LT.FRETOP,
0000:              ; 			[MINPTR]=THIS STRING DESCRIPTOR'S POINTER,
0000:              ; 			[REMMIN]=POINTER AT THIS STRING DESCRIPTOR.
0000:              ; 			END.
0000:              ; 			3. IF REMMIN.NE.0 (WE FOUND AN UNCOLLECTED STRING),
0000:              ; 			BLOCK TRANSFER THE STRING DATA POINTED
0000:              ; 			TO IN THE STRING DESCRIPTOR POINTED TO BY "REMMIN"
0000:              ; 			SO THAT THE LAST BYTE OF STRING DATA IS AT
0000:              ; 			[FRETOP]. UPDATE [FRETOP] SO THAT IT
0000:              ; 			POINTS TO THE LOCATION JUST BELOW THE ONE
0000:              ; 			THE STRING DATA WAS MOVED INTO. UPDATE
0000:              ; 			THE POINTER IN THE DESCRIPTOR SO IT POINTS
0000:              ; 			TO THE NEW LOCATION OF THE STRING DATA.
0000:              ; 			GO TO STEP 1.
0000:              
0000:              ; 		AFTER CALLING GARBAGE COLLECTION "GETSPA" AGAIN CHECKS
0000:              ; 		TO SEE IF [ACCA] CHARACTERS ARE AVAILABLE BETWEEN
0000:              ; 		[STREND] AND [FRETOP]; IF NOT, AN "OUT OF STRING"
0000:              ; 		ERROR IS INVOKED.
0000:              
0000:              ; 	MATH PACKAGE
0000:              ; 		THE MATH PACKAGE CONTAINS FLOATING INPUT (FIN),
0000:              ; 		FLOATING OUTPUT (FOUT), FLOATING COMPARE (FCOMP)
0000:              ; 		... AND ALL THE NUMERIC OPERATORS AND FUNCTIONS.
0000:              ; 		THE FORMATS, CONVENTIONS AND ENTRY POINTS ARE ALL
0000:              ; 		DESCRIBED IN THE MATH PACKAGE ITSELF.
0000:              
0000:              ; 	INIT -- THE INITIALIZATION ROUTINE
0000:              ; 		THE AMOUNT OF MEMORY,
0000:              ; 		TERMINAL WIDTH, AND WHICH FUNCTIONS TO BE RETAINED
0000:              ; 		ARE ASCERTAINED FROM THE USER. A ZERO IS PUT DOWN
0000:              ; 		AT THE FIRST LOCATION NOT USED BY THE MATH-PACKAGE
0000:              ; 		AND [TXTTAB] IS SET UP TO POINT AT THE NEXT LOCATION.
0000:              ; 		THIS DETERMINES WHERE PROGRAM STORAGE WILL START.
0000:              ; 		SPECIAL CHECKS ARE MADE TO MAKE SURE
0000:              ; 		ALL QUESTIONS IN "INIT" ARE ANSWERED REASONABLY, SINCE
0000:              ; 		ONCE "INIT" FINISHES, THE LOCATIONS IT USES ARE
0000:              ; 		USED FOR PROGRAM STORAGE. THE LAST THING "INIT" DOES IS
0000:              ; 		CHANGE LOCATION ZERO TO BE A JUMP TO "READY" INSTEAD
0000:              ; 		OF "INIT". ONCE THIS IS DONE THERE IS NO WAY TO RESTART
0000:              ; 		"INIT".
0000:              ; HIGH LOCATIONS

